tain muscles we  need to relearn it when we do it with  the stimulation it's not that natural  but it becomes more and more natural the  more you train ali  the researchers are quick to point out  their discovery isn't a miracle or a  cure nor is it a surgical fix for spinal  injuries that are still considered  irreparable but they believe with work  it can improve quality of life at the  very beginning  it's not super easy they still need  weight bearing and they need to  understand what's going on  but they can activate immediately their  legs  and and step a trial launch is planned  in the u.s next year with up to 100  patients by then rocati hopes to have  achieved his goal of walking a kilometer  on his own with the walker  i'm free i can walk wherever i want  in this moment  they told me okay now i'm working  microlight global news toronto  an armchair army of gamers helping  scientists solve the biggest mysteries  in medicine next  gamers are skilled at saving fictional  worlds but during the pandemic some have  been given a chance to play a game that  could have an impact on the real world  fight against covet  john hua has more on the program and its  impact john  when the pandemic hit a gaming company  knew it wanted to help in any way it  could  and it had a secret weapon an armchair  army it started here in reykjavik  iceland with the gaming company ccp the  makers of the hugely popular game eve  online they knew with the number of  players in computing power at their  fingertips that could lend a hand in the  fight against coven 19.  so they reached out to the medical  community and were put in touch with  ryan brinkman a scientist at the bc  cancer research center with his help  they were able to create a game where  players try to identify the different  effects of covid on human cells we're  looking for an increase in the amount of  certain cells or a decrease in the  amount of certain cells  the first cell samples used in the game  were taken from patients in northern  italy to have it come in right from  those front lines was a bit of a surreal  thing if you really thought about it  enough but it also was you know a  motivator for us to really push this  thing out  it asks players to try and identify  different cell types using real cell  samples from covet patients  it was something that particularly  appealed to bc gamer mike daw  when project discovery came out for the  covid research i was just about to start  chemotherapy so this was near and dear  to my heart i felt like i was getting  involved with the science that was  affecting the whole planet  in the flow cytometer game players like  michael are tasked with lassoing a group  of dots they believe are the same type  of cell  i can definitely see that there is a  cluster here so i'm doing my best to  outline it  the mini game called project discovery  flow cytometer created within eve online  has been a massive success  gamers have done the equivalent of over  510 years of work identifying over 13  million samples  and all the data they have provided will  be used to create a program to save  scientists even more time in the future  it's information that can help  scientists not only understand covid but  other diseases like cancer  thank you john and you can watch john's  full report tonight at 8 on the new  reality right here on global  and next on global national the 97 year  old swimmer setting records just as  impressive as anything you'll see at the  olympics and her next big goal  betty brussell has loved swimming all  her life but it wasn't until she was in  her 60s that she realized she was really  good at it now 97 she's won countless  medals and broken two world records and  as jay duran shows us betty isn't even  close to the finish line yet  i don't know where these are from you'll  have to forgive betty brussel it's hard  to keep track two fourth she has a bag  full of swim metal she can barely lift  most of them are gold she's usually  standing on top of the podium but what's  really impressive is the fact she didn't  start swimming competitively until she  was 68. i am a late bloomer yes betty  grew up with her 11 siblings just north  of amsterdam that's her on the right  they learned to swim in this canal  taking the plunge any chance they could  get going out at seven o'clock in the  morning before i went to work  and just swam into canal and it man it  was cold  she could never have guessed that one  day she would set two world records at  masters meets pretty good i was quite  happy with that  [Applause]  [Music]  that was really really nice  life's getting busy for betty again  twice a week she drives herself to the  pool to train  there's still a lot of work to do her  goal in life is to make it to a hundred  and to break all the world records in  the 100 to 104 age group  she has suffered a heart attack broke  both feet cracked a vertebra and a bad  fall and had an operation on her  shoulder none of it has stopped her i  always when i'm recovered i go back  i go to rest a little bit too you know  an old lady  quiet time is a little more typical for  someone her age her other hobby isn't  nearly as demanding  but there are still medals to win and  records to break just in case you were  wondering how much longer betty brussell  will keep coming to the pool always  always i feel them  i feel always the best when i'm swimming  jay durant global news  keep on swimming betty that is global  national for this saturday night i'm  chris galas tonight your canada is a  frozen belmont lake in havelock ontario  thanks for watching we'll see you back  here tomorrow night  [Music]  you
 

